{
  "behavior": {
    "name": "Mutual Product Convergence with Curved Path",
    "description": "A unique, non-communicative strategy that centers on shared minimize-the-sum-of-pairs logic and curved, synchronized approach. This behavior treats both agents as teammates aiming for the same optimal landmark by balancing collective effort and smooth convergence.\n\nAlgorithm sketch:\n- Landmark selection (shared target): For every landmark L, compute D_self = distance(ego_agent_position, L) and D_other = distance(other_agent_position, L). Score_L = D_self * D_other. Pick L* with the smallest Score_L; in case of ties, break by smallest x, then smallest y. This minimizes the total pairwise travel effort and tends to pick a landmark that\u2019s reasonably accessible to both.\n- Movement direction (curved pursuit): dir_to_L = normalize(L* - ego_agent_position). perp = rotate90(dir_to_L). Curvature factor c = clamp((D_other - D_self) / (max(D_self, D_other) + 1e-6), -1, 1) * curvature_scale. The movement heading is Move_dir = normalize(dir_to_L + c * perp). This creates a gentle curve toward the target, reducing head-on race tendencies and avoiding symmetry stalemates.\n- Speed shaping (balanced pacing): base_speed = max_speed * 0.65. speed_factor = 0.4 + 0.6 * (D_other / (D_self + D_other + 1e-6)). velocity_mag = clamp(base_speed * speed_factor, 0, max_speed). Set ego_agent_velocity = velocity_mag * Move_dir.\n- Arrival handling: If D_self <= arrival_threshold, set ego_agent_velocity = (0, 0). If both D_self <= arrival_threshold and D_other <= arrival_threshold, hold position (allowing graceful completion when possible).\n\nRationale and personality:\n- Shared landmark selection via the product of distances encourages a landmark that\u2019s efficiently reachable for both agents, aligning goals without any communication.\n- The curved approach subtly biases the ego toward approach from a side relative to the other agent, promoting robust coordination in symmetric environments and reducing the risk of locking into a straight-line stalemate.\n- Adaptive speed ensures the agent slows if the other is close and speeds up when the other is farther away, promoting more balanced arrival times.\n- Arrival policy provides a polite handoff by stopping when close enough, allowing the partner to finish if needed and enabling near-simultaneous completion when feasible."
